<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of York</h1><p class="st-meta">875 CE – 954 CE (approx.) · capital York (Jorvik)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Viking kingdom centred on Jorvik, a prosperous Anglo-Scandinavian town whose kings were often also rulers of Dublin.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> captured York in 866 to 867, and in 876 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halfdan-ragnarsson">Halfdan</a> shared out the land of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Northumbria</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tees">Tees</a> among his followers. Danish kings ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guthfrith-of-york">Guthfrith</a> in the 880s accepting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Early in the 10th century the Norse dynasty of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-dublin">Kingdom of Dublin</a> took over.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">Jorvik</a> grew into a major town of craftsmen and traders, with goods from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coppergate">Coppergate</a> excavations show. Its kings minted coins, some with Christian symbols, and cooperated with the archbishops of York. Scandinavian settlement left hundreds of place-names across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Aethelstan seized York in 927, but Norse kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-guthfrithson">Olaf Guthfrithson</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-cuaran">Amlaib Cuaran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-bloodaxe">Eric Bloodaxe</a> returned repeatedly. Eric's expulsion and death in 954 ended the kingdom, and York became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England</a> as an earldom.</p></div>